WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.095
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.395 --> 00:00:08.479
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.779 --> 00:00:15.100
Unit ten: Urban Development. Today you will weigh the benefits and costs of urban change with 'although' clauses.

4
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.180
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:22.273
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.117 --> 00:00:24.362
Urbanization.

7
00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:29.335
Urbanization means the growth of cities as people move from rural areas.

8
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:33.111
Rapid urbanization has transformed Phnom Penh.

9
00:00:33.717 --> 00:00:36.682
Your turn. Say: Urbanization.

10
00:00:39.533 --> 00:00:40.627
Urbanization.

11
00:00:41.383 --> 00:00:42.628
Infrastructure.

12
00:00:43.617 --> 00:00:47.300
Infrastructure means basic systems such as roads, water and power.

13
00:00:47.883 --> 00:00:50.719
New infrastructure reduced flooding in the city.

14
00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:54.097
Your turn. Say: Infrastructure.

15
00:00:56.933 --> 00:00:58.236
Infrastructure.

16
00:00:58.983 --> 00:00:59.833
Zoning.

17
00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:04.533
Zoning means rules about how land in different areas may be used.

18
00:01:05.117 --> 00:01:07.755
Zoning laws separate factories from homes.

19
00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:10.689
Your turn. Say: Zoning.

20
00:01:13.533 --> 00:01:14.488
Zoning.

21
00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:16.530
Gentrification.

22
00:01:17.517 --> 00:01:22.964
Gentrification means the renovation of poor areas, often pushing out original residents.

23
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:26.362
Gentrification raised rents along the riverside.

24
00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:29.776
Your turn. Say: Gentrification.

25
00:01:32.633 --> 00:01:33.983
Gentrification.

26
00:01:34.717 --> 00:01:36.089
Public space.

27
00:01:37.083 --> 00:01:41.080
Public space means areas open to everyone, such as parks and squares.

28
00:01:41.683 --> 00:01:44.263
Public space encourages community life.

29
00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.618
Your turn. Say: Public space.

30
00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:51.735
Public space.

31
00:01:52.483 --> 00:01:53.403
Density.

32
00:01:54.417 --> 00:01:57.542
Density means the number of people living in an area.

33
00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:01.032
High density can make public transport efficient.

34
00:02:01.617 --> 00:02:04.304
Your turn. Say: Density.

35
00:02:07.167 --> 00:02:08.110
Density.

36
00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:09.712
Planning.

37
00:02:10.717 --> 00:02:13.750
Planning means deciding how land and cities will develop.

38
00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:16.768
Good planning prevents traffic chaos.

39
00:02:17.383 --> 00:02:19.989
Your turn. Say: Planning.

40
00:02:22.833 --> 00:02:23.625
Planning.

41
00:02:24.383 --> 00:02:25.559
Displacement.

42
00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:30.047
Displacement means being forced to leave one's home or area.

43
00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:34.101
The project caused the displacement of 4,000 families.

44
00:02:34.717 --> 00:02:37.415
Your turn. Say: Displacement.

45
00:02:40.267 --> 00:02:41.303
Displacement.

46
00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:45.461
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:46.317 --> 00:02:51.430
Visal, the lake in the centre of Phnom Penh was filled in for development. Was that progress?

48
00:02:52.517 --> 00:03:00.298
Although the project brought modern buildings and investment, thousands of residents were displaced with little compensation.

49
00:03:01.383 --> 00:03:03.580
Couldn't the city have balanced both?

50
00:03:04.683 --> 00:03:15.265
Better planning might have helped. Although gentrification revitalizes an area, it should include affordable housing for the original community.

51
00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.071
What else did the city lose?

52
00:03:19.183 --> 00:03:28.337
Public space and flood protection. Even though land is valuable, lakes absorb rainwater, so some areas now flood more often.

53
00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:31.221
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:32.083 --> 00:03:35.035
Grammar focus: Although plus contrast.

55
00:03:35.335 --> 00:03:36.580
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:42.034
Although gentrification revitalizes an area, it can displace residents.

57
00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:46.784
Although the project brought investment, thousands were displaced.

58
00:03:47.683 --> 00:03:51.146
Even though land is valuable, lakes protect cities from floods.

59
00:03:52.100 --> 00:04:01.892
Use 'although' + clause to concede a point before presenting your main argument in the main clause. 'Despite / in spite of' need a noun or -ing: 'Despite the investment, …'.

60
00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:06.626
Be careful! Don't say: Although it helps, but it displaces people.

61
00:04:08.567 --> 00:04:11.576
Say: Although it helps, it displaces people.

62
00:04:11.876 --> 00:04:13.574
Don't use 'but' after 'although'.

63
00:04:14.367 --> 00:04:16.880
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:17.667 --> 00:04:18.784
Cities for Whom?.

65
00:04:19.084 --> 00:04:26.099
More than half of the world's population now lives in cities, and the proportion is rising fastest in Asia and Africa.

66
00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:34.220
Although urbanization creates jobs and opportunities, it often outpaces the construction of essential infrastructure.

67
00:04:35.233 --> 00:04:39.868
In many cities, informal settlements have been cleared to make way for commercial projects.

68
00:04:40.867 --> 00:04:47.081
Planners increasingly argue that development should protect public space, natural drainage and affordable housing.

69
00:04:48.067 --> 00:04:52.562
The central question is not whether cities should grow, but who benefits when they do.

70
00:04:53.667 --> 00:04:57.378
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:58.217 --> 00:05:01.830
blank the city grew richer, many residents became poorer.

72
00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:05.505
A, Although. B, Despite. C, However.

73
00:05:09.067 --> 00:05:10.869
The answer is A: Although.

74
00:05:12.017 --> 00:05:14.678
blank the new roads, traffic remained heavy.

75
00:05:14.978 --> 00:05:18.736
A, Although. B, Despite. C, Even though.

76
00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:24.741
The answer is B: Despite.

77
00:05:25.883 --> 00:05:28.080
Being forced to leave one's home is blank.

78
00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:32.359
A, density. B, zoning. C, displacement.

79
00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:37.969
The answer is C: displacement.

80
00:05:39.117 --> 00:05:42.289
Basic systems like roads, water and power are blank.

81
00:05:42.589 --> 00:05:46.683
A, infrastructure. B, public space. C, planning.

82
00:05:50.233 --> 00:05:52.860
The answer is A: infrastructure.

83
00:05:54.017 --> 00:05:57.050
According to the reading, what is the central question?

84
00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:03.847
A, whether cities should grow. B, who benefits when cities grow. C, how tall buildings can be.

85
00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:10.433
The answer is B: who benefits when cities grow.

86
00:06:11.633 --> 00:06:14.913
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:19.131
What is one tradeoff between urban development and displacement?

88
00:06:35.917 --> 00:06:37.405
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:37.705 --> 00:07:14.244
One tradeoff is that development brings investment and modern housing while forcing poorer residents to leave the area. Although renovated neighbourhoods often become safer and cleaner, rising rents mean that the original community can no longer afford to live there. In Phnom Penh, for example, several lake communities were relocated far from their jobs. Supporters argue that cities must modernise to compete. I agree, but I believe fair compensation and affordable housing should be part of every project.

90
00:07:14.544 --> 00:07:16.450
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:21.570
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and although plus contrast.

92
00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:28.307
Today's value is community awareness. Notice how changes affect every neighbour, not only the winners.

93
00:07:28.607 --> 00:07:31.120
See you in Unit eleven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:34.571
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

